    "available_at": "2026-10-29T01:24:18.963129+00:00",
    "body": "By Fleet Hero\n29 October 2026\nThere is a moment every flatbed driver knows well. You are standing in the yard, coffee in hand, looking at a load that needs to move by morning. All the necessary American truck parts are packed: the tarp is folded on the catwalk, and the straps are coiled and ready. Everything looks fine. Then you realize you never actually measured the load. You guessed. And that guess is about to cost you money, time, or both.\nIt happens every single day across the country. Drivers skip the tape because they have hauled similar loads before. They assume the height is close enough. They figure the width will be fine. That assumption travels with them onto the highway, and it does not end well.\nUse American Truck Parts Properly: The Tarp Will Tell You the Truth Eventually\nThe tarp isn’t some soft blanket that allows for mistakes in measuring. The tarp is cut to exact specifications. Should you lay the tarp over cargo which is taller than you anticipated, the tarp will pull tight on the edges, while the center drops down. Wind gets inside that loose fabric almost right away. At sixty-five miles per hour, that tarp flapping against the trailer becomes something which will batter the stitching of the tarp itself.\nStraps Lose Their Grip When Angles Change\nEvery strap in American truck parts has a working load limit. That limit assumes a specific angle between the strap and the cargo. When the load is taller or wider than planned, that angle changes. A strap that should run at forty-five degrees might end up nearly vertical. Vertical straps lose holding power. Tension concentrates on one point instead of spreading across the load. Webbing stretches. Hardware bends. The cargo shifts.\nCorner protectors are supposed to prevent this by creating a smooth surface for the strap to slide across. But if the dimensions are wrong, the protectors sit in the wrong place. The strap rubs directly against rough cargo. Friction eats through the webbing. A strap that should last years fails in one trip.\nPermits Are Legal Documents, Not Suggestions\nOversize and overweight permits list exact dimensions of American truck parts. If you apply for a permit with a height of fourteen feet, that permit covers a load measuring fourteen feet. Not fourteen feet six inches. Not fifteen feet. The number on the paper has to match the number on the road.\nBridge strikes in New York reached 350 in 2024, according to the governor’s office , which is why its “Check Your Height” campaign is now running in 16 states and Washington, D.C., and why it warns that consumer-grade GPS can steer trucks onto low-clearance routes. A navigation app cannot know how tall a tarped load really is, so the measurement has to come from a tape, not a screen.\nThat warning applies everywhere.\nBuilding Better Habits Behind the Wheel\nMeasure height with the tarp installed. Measure width at the widest point, including overhang. Measure length for the full combination. Scale the load and record axle weights. Write everything down. Recheck after any adjustment.\nThese steps take five minutes. A bridge strike takes hours. A rejected permit takes days. A load that shifts because the straps were never going to hold takes lives.\nMeasurement is not glamorous work. Nobody brags about measuring a load correctly. But it is the work that keeps tarps intact, straps tight, and permits valid. Drivers who treat measurement as a core part of the job spend less time fixing problems on the shoulder and more time delivering loads the way they were planned.\nThis article was sponsored by Fleet Hero.",
